(7755320, 1282831, 177030, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Neat:</b></div><hr/><i> Seriously Don 100%, absolutely fantastic photo!<br/> <br/>Adding as a favourite. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Anita, from the Queen of Silhouettes, Im just flattered by your comments. Thank you!  107 attempts. I had several with a single rider I was quite content with. I didnt even know I got this one until I downloaded the images!  Pleasantly surprised to say the least. This a full frame image, no cropping. 100 degree temps and I had to walk to the spot and didnt want to lug a second lens;  Figured Id be better off with the 70-200, wrong.  At 70 mm, my back was against a barbed wire fence with six foot dense weeds behind it.  <br/> <br/>Sunset was around 8:30 pm, and the riders had to be off the track by 8:30, so I wasnt going to get the golden light I wanted. This one was snapped at around 7. Im at the bottom of a 20 feet hill, so the illusion of sunset is there. I did have to play with this to get the light color I wanted. I actually used your latest entry as a guide. Its a natural vignette, not added. Exposure was really tricky because I could not time very well where the riders would be with the sun and they were very fast.  Ended up with a high shutter speed priority with an automatic 5 stops underexposure dialed in. Most of the riders had called it a day by 7:30 and were leaving. <br/> <br/>Thanks everyone for the wonderful comments. I know now where the higher scores come from. Wish I could figure out the 4s and 5s.</td>', 'Originally posted by Neat: Seriously Don 100%, absolutely fantastic photo! Adding as a favourite.   Anita, from the Queen of Silhouettes, Im just flattered by your comments. Thank you!  107 attempts. I had several with a single rider I was quite content with. I didnt even know I got this one until I downloaded the images!  Pleasantly surprised to say the least. This a full frame image, no cropping. 100 degree temps and I had to walk to the spot and didnt want to lug a second lens;  Figured Id be better off with the 70-200, wrong.  At 70 mm, my back was against a barbed wire fence with six foot dense weeds behind it.   Sunset was around 8:30 pm, and the riders had to be off the track by 8:30, so I wasnt going to get the golden light I wanted. This one was snapped at around 7. Im at the bottom of a 20 feet hill, so the illusion of sunset is there. I did have to play with this to get the light color I wanted. I actually used your latest entry as a guide. Its a natural vignette, not added. Exposure was really tricky because I could not time very well where the riders would be with the sun and they were very fast.  Ended up with a high shutter speed priority with an automatic 5 stops underexposure dialed in. Most of the riders had called it a day by 7:30 and were leaving.  Thanks everyone for the wonderful comments. I know now where the higher scores come from. Wish I could figure out the 4s and 5s.', '2023-07-31 12:41:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(7755324, 1282639, 34171, '<td valign="top">This was once of my favorites!</td>', 'This was once of my favorites!', '2023-07-31 13:10:24.000000', NULL, 0),
